=> Think of CPU like workers 

Suppose you have a shop.

1 worker → can work on 1 task at a time
4 workers → can work on multiple tasks at the same time

In Linux, these "workers" are basically the CPU processing units that the operating system can use.

=> When you run:

nproc

Linux tells you:

"How many CPU processing units can I use?"

=> Example 1

You run:

nproc

Output:

4

It means:

Your Linux system has 4 CPU processing units available.

You can imagine:

CPU
├── Worker 1
├── Worker 2
├── Worker 3
└── Worker 4

So Linux can distribute work among these 4 processing units.

=> Note:- For CPU usage, we use commands/tools such as:

top

or

htop
